# Imperfect Land — An Adventure Uncovering the Secrets of the Perfect Land Sect

**Version:** 0.5.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game — Imperfect Land
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

## Imperfect Land

Imperfect Land is an adventure for the Legend of the Five Rings Roleplaying Game. It places the characters at the heart of a religious conflict: as debate over the unorthodox and controversial Perfect Land Sect threatens to tear the Brotherhood of Shinsei apart, the PCs have a chance to shape the spiritual future of Rokugan—and their own futures as well.

This adventure is best suited to characters who have opinions on the sect, whether positive or negative. Dragon and Phoenix PCs are ideal for the story, because their clans are at the forefront of the argument, as are spiritual characters like shugenja (priests born of the samurai caste) and monks. However, any PC with an interest in the subject can be involved. If this adventure is incorporated into an ongoing campaign, the GM may wish to engineer one or more encounters with adherents of the Perfect Land Sect prior to the adventure’s gathering of the Brotherhood, building up a history to shape the players’ decisions. It is strongly recommended that the GM read through Imperfect Land in its entirety to become familiar with the cast and storylines before running it with a group of PCs.

### Adventure Overview

Imperfect Land begins with the leaders of the Brotherhood’s various orders gathering at the Mountain Song Temple in Dragon lands (described on page 76 of Writ of the Wilds), under the hospitality of Dragon family daimyō Mirumoto Masashige. The Brotherhood holds such a conclave each year and conducts other points of business during the event, but everyone is aware that this year’s main agenda item is a vote on whether to formally bestow the label of False Path on the Perfect Land Sect. Failure to achieve a sufficient degree of consensus in one direction or the other would mean the Brotherhood would risk falling into a violent schism. Masashige requests the PCs help with the gathering, acting as guards, clerks, or other assistants, depending on their skills and backgrounds.

In Part One, the PCs have a chance to sway the vote toward support for or opposition to the Perfect Land Sect. Their duties to the conclave give them many opportunities to speak with the leaders who are still undecided, using a variety of tactics to make those leaders more receptive to their arguments.

In Part Two, a delegation of Perfect Land adherents arrives, including their leader, Hige. This provides an opportunity to investigate the origins of the sect more closely, gives rise to a familial confrontation, and presents a chance to uncover a spy.

In Part Three, an eruption of the elemental imbalance brings the constant melody of the Mountain Song to an abrupt halt, possibly in response to the strife of the conclave. The PCs are tasked to investigate, which takes them outside the temple to a tengu ruin, where they may confront a variety of enemies to save a stranger.

In Part Four, the final segment of this adventure, the PCs return to the conclave, thwart an assassination attempt, and help decide the ultimate fate of the Perfect Land Sect—along with that of Rokugan itself.

#### The Perfect Land

The origins of the controversial Perfect Land Sect lie in the late ninth century, when Yuzue, a young monk who belonged to the Shrine of the Seven Thunders, theorized that the Little Teacher’s conversation with Hantei had begun the Age of Celestial Virtue in Rokugan: a period lasting eight centuries (one hundred years for each founding deity who heard his teachings), during which the Empire prospered and was spirituality strong.

But that age had ended, and according to Yuzue, Rokugan was suffering in the Suijindai, the Age of Declining Virtue. Corruption was undermining the Celestial Order, the Tao itself insufficient to free people from the shackles of rebirth. Because of this, Yuzue prayed ceaselessly to Shinsei, beseeching his return, with the mantra Shoshi ni kie (variously translated as “devotion to the Little Teacher” or “absolute trust in the Little Teacher”).

The sect as an organized movement came into existence only after Yuzue’s passing, thanks to the efforts of her student Gatai. The sutra she allegedly wrote before her death described a realm within the Celestial Court, the Perfect Land, which Shinsei had created to help the suffering souls of Rokugan. Those who chant the kie are not subject to rebirth according to their karma: instead; their souls go to the Perfect Land, where they can work toward Enlightenment under the guidance of the Little Teacher himself. Following the teachings of this sutra, Gatai gathered followers, and the sect grew rapidly in Phoenix lands.

To more orthodox minds, however, the Perfect Land Sect’s teachings are heresy. The key difference lies in the concepts of jiriki and tariki, “self-power” and “other-power.” Mainstream Shinseism is founded on the idea of jiriki, holding that Enlightenment is achieved through one’s own efforts via meditation, ascetic practices, the study of esoteric texts, and other methods. Tariki means relying on the assistance of an outside power—in this case, Shinsei—to reach that end. According to the founding sutra of the Perfect Land, while jiriki was sufficient during the Age of Celestial Virtue, in the Age of Declining Virtue, it has become too difficult for most souls to achieve liberation on their own. Shinsei therefore created the Perfect Land and the kie out of his boundless compassion and his desire to relieve the suffering of people in the Mortal Realm.

